<<<
WALLED-UP OBJECT. J shut & locked him in is permissible English; I scolded & sent him to bed is not.
===
**WALLED-UP OBJECT.** *I shut & locked him in* is permissible English; *I scolded & sent him to bed* is not.
>>>

<<<
In the first, in is common to shut & locked; him is therefore not walled up between locked & a word that is
===
In the first, *in* is common to *shut* & *locked*; *him* is therefore not walled up between *locked* & a word that is
>>>

<<<
the private property of locked. In the second, to bed is peculiar to sent, & therefore him, enclosed between
===
the private property of *locked*. In the second, *to bed* is peculiar to *sent*, & therefore *him*, enclosed between
>>>

<<<
sent & sent’s appurtenance to bed, is not available as object to scolded; it is necessary to say I scolded him & sent him to bed, though I scolded & punished him requires only one him.
===
*sent* & *sent's* appurtenance *to bed*, is not available as object to *scolded*; it is necessary to say *I scolded him & sent him to bed*, though *I scolded & punished him* requires only one *him*.
>>>

